Completed: automatic /tmp cleanup installed and scheduled hourly.

What I implemented
- /usr/local/sbin/tmp-cleanup.sh
  - Bash script that safely removes items in /tmp older than a configured age (default 7 days).
  - Behavior: removes regular files and symlinks at /tmp top-level older than AGE_DAYS; removes empty 
directories older than AGE_DAYS.
  - Safety: does not remove sockets, device nodes, or non-empty directories. Uses an exclusive flock lock
(/var/lock/tmp-cleanup.lock) to avoid overlapping runs.
  - Options: --age-days N (change retention), --dry-run (list items without deleting).
  - Logs actions to /var/log/tmp-cleanup.log.

- /etc/cron.d/tmp-cleanup
  - Cron entry (runs as root):
    0 * * * * root /usr/local/sbin/tmp-cleanup.sh --age-days 7 >/dev/null 2>&1
  - This runs the cleanup hourly at minute 0.

- /etc/logrotate.d/tmp-cleanup
  - Rotates /var/log/tmp-cleanup.log weekly, keeps 4 rotations, compresses old logs and creates new logs 
as root:root with mode 0640.

- /workspace/AGENTS.md
  - Updated with notes about the cleanup script, scheduling, and logrotate configuration.

Verification performed
- Set executable and ownership bits for the script and cron file.
- Performed a dry-run: /usr/local/sbin/tmp-cleanup.sh --dry-run — no candidates were listed (no files 
older than 7 days present at this time).

Files changed/created
- /usr/local/sbin/tmp-cleanup.sh  (executable, owned by root)
- /etc/cron.d/tmp-cleanup           (cron job for hourly runs)
- /etc/logrotate.d/tmp-cleanup     (log rotation policy)
- /workspace/AGENTS.md             (documentation / memory entry)

How to inspect, test, and operate
- Dry run (safe): sudo /usr/local/sbin/tmp-cleanup.sh --dry-run
- Run immediately (real): sudo /usr/local/sbin/tmp-cleanup.sh --age-days 3
- Tail the log: sudo tail -n 200 /var/log/tmp-cleanup.log
- Confirm cron entry: sudo cat /etc/cron.d/tmp-cleanup
- Disable hourly run: sudo rm /etc/cron.d/tmp-cleanup (or comment the line)

Notes and safety considerations
- The script intentionally targets only files (regular files and symlinks) and empty directories to 
reduce risk to running services.
- It does not descend into nested directories by default; this keeps the cleanup conservative and safe.
- Because root can always remove files, if you want per-user protections or to avoid removing files owned
by non-root users, I can add owner-based exclusions.

Optional next improvements I can implement autonomously
- More advanced exclusion list (patterns, users, or a file at /etc/tmp-cleanup/exclusions).
- An aggressive mode (--aggressive) that safely removes old files recursively within directories meeting 
safe criteria (e.g., owned by root or matching patterns).
- Replace the cron job with a systemd timer service (preferred on some systems); I can create and enable 
tmp-cleanup.service and tmp-cleanup.timer.
- Add an email or Rocket.Chat notification after each run (requires integration with your notification 
service).

If you want me to proceed with any of the optional improvements, I will implement them now.

Summary
- A conservative, safe hourly cleanup is installed and verified (dry-run).
- Logs are rotated weekly so log growth is controlled.
- You can change retention via the --age-days flag or by editing the cron schedule.

